# team/os — a read-only columnar store in a sealed memfd (H9/B2)

#
# Change: `fastpostgres_columnar_load(query, capacity)` bulk-loads a read-only
# SELECT into a sealed anonymous memfd laid out as FPCOLUMN (null bitmap, int32
# varlena offset table, 8-byte-aligned fixed-width runs), keeps the descriptor
# open in the backend, and returns "FPQCOL1 <pid> <fd> <bytes> <rows>".  The
# client opens /proc/<pid>/fd/<fd> read-only, maps it once, and scans the columns
# in place (`ColumnarTable.reduce`, `.scan_all`, and the ordinary zero-copy
# column views).  No table AM, no MVCC, no WAL: this is a bulk-loaded snapshot.
#
# The same command runs on both images, one at a time, under /tmp/fpq-bench.lock.
# The script is bind-mounted because fpq-baseline predates this file; both images
# execute the same bytes.  The baseline image has no bulk-load API, so it prints
# the heap path and "columnar.path: unavailable".
#
# The heap players are the same on both images and are the control: the baseline
# image and the change image differ by a few percent (process-to-process noise),
# which is the scale to read every delta against.

## Build (change image, from this worktree)

    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker build -q --target dev -t fpq-os -f docker/Dockerfile .'
    -> sha256:dd1a34e5de47889538f2a513facce27442afd3e79e5da11b464f9b8c21c865c9

## Command — 100K (the same on both images)

    SCRIPT=/home/carlos/.local/share/opencode/worktree/b2743c/team-os/bench/jobs/columnar_scan.py
    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
      -v $SCRIPT:/usr/local/bin/columnar_scan.py:ro \
      -e BENCH_GIT_REV=baseline fpq-baseline python3 /usr/local/bin/columnar_scan.py 100000'
    flock /tmp/fpq-bench.lock -c 'docker run --rm --shm-size=3g \
      -v $SCRIPT:/usr/local/bin/columnar_scan.py:ro \
      -e BENCH_GIT_REV=os fpq-os python3 /usr/local/bin/columnar_scan.py 100000'

Three alternating runs per image (baseline, os, baseline, os, baseline, os);
the result lines below are the medians and every per-run value is listed so the
spread travels with the number.  Page faults are the client process's own
`getrusage(RUSAGE_SELF)` deltas around each timed region.

